. /* Interpretation: 
> The most important thing to note is that the coefficient for the dummy imp_un
> ilateral (=1 for years when the laws already passed), changes across specific
> ations: for the first one, it is negative and non-significant. This changes a
> s we take into account linear time trend for each state, as the new coefficie
> nt for impo_unilateral is positive and significant at any conventional level 
> (it has value 0.47). 
> As the first specification was forcing all states to have the same coefficien
> t (so the same effect of the laws passing), this was likely creating a bias w
> hich is not present anymore as we allow for state and year interactions. 
> Also in the third specification imp_unilateral is significant, and it remains
>  like that in the third specification (0.33, significant at the 0.05 level). 
> Again, this suggests that taking into account  quadratic time trends as well 
> allows to get a more precise estimate (indeed some quatratic time trends are 
> significant). The coefficient in the third specificaiton is smaller, suggesti
> ng that not allowing for quadratic time trend leads to an overestimation bias
> . 
> 
> Overall, the reason why the results change across specification seems to be t
> hat different states are on different trends, therefore suggesting a violatio
> n of the parallel trend assumption: they don't only have different intercepts
>  (as it is captured by significant coefficient of many states fixed effects i
> n the first specification), but different slopes when we regress  divorce rat
> es on having passed divoce laws. This might suggest that the parallel trend a
> ssumption doesn't hold. 
> On the contrary, if the parallel trend assumption holds, the results for the 
> imp_unilateral coefficient should be consistent. */
. 
. *POINT F
. *create simulated observations
. clear

. set obs 6
Number of observations (_N) was 0, now 6.

. gen obs = _n 

. gen state = floor(.9 + obs/3 ) 

. bysort state: gen year = _n 

. gen D = (state == 1 & year == 3) 

. replace D = 1 if state == 2 & (year == 2 | year == 3)
(2 real changes made)

. 
. *create simulated outcomes
. set seed 1111

. gen Y = 0.1 + 0.02*(year==2) + 0.05*(D==1) + runiform()/100

. gen Y2 = 0.1 + 0.02*(year==2) + 0.05*(D==1) + 0.3*(state == 2 & year == 3) + 
> runiform()/100

. gen Y3 = 0.1 + 0.02*(year==2) + 0.05*(D==1) + 0.4*(state == 2 & year == 3) + 
> runiform()/100

. gen Y4 = 0.1 + 0.02*(year==2) + 0.05*(D==1) + 0.5*(state == 2 & year == 3) + 
> runiform()/100

. 
. *regress all Ys on state and year dummies + a treatment dummy 
. xtset state year 

