. /*when we use the first Y, there is a positive tratment effect that is specif
> ied in the Y equation, and that it is correctly identifies in the regression 
> (here the coefficient is 0.049, very close to the theoretical 0.05, significa
> nt at any conventional size). 
> However, under the Y2,3,4 specification, we add an additional positive compon
> ent in period 3  for state 2: this can be interpreted as a boost of the treat
> ment effect in the second period of treatment, or as something specific to st
> ate 2. in any case, we end up with heterogeneous treatment effect.
> We easily see that the coefficient estimation for D, even if it remains signi
> ficant, gets wrong in all of the remaining regressions. It is negative, and t
> he bias increases the bigger the shock in period 3 becomes (note it goes from
>  0.3 to 0.5). 
> With staggered implementetion and heterogeneous treatment effect, we no longe
> r compare a treated and an untreated unit: already treated units are used as 
> controls for later-treated units. These simple regressions show how if there 
> are time trends that we do not take into account across states, the resulting
>  coefficients for the treatment can get biased, making it impossible to estim
> ate the treatment coefficient consistenly. */ 
. 
. *POINT G
. *keep balanced panel 
. twowayfeweights Y state year D, type(feTR)
twowayfeweights requires the gtools package: ssc install gtools, replace

. twowayfeweights Y2 state year D, type(feTR)
twowayfeweights requires the gtools package: ssc install gtools, replace

. twowayfeweights Y3 state year D, type(feTR)
twowayfeweights requires the gtools package: ssc install gtools, replace

. twowayfeweights Y4 state year D, type(feTR)
twowayfeweights requires the gtools package: ssc install gtools, replace

. 
. /* The TWFE estimand is the sum of the ATT for the specific (state, year) wit
> h weights that sum to one. The coefficient for D in the regression is the sam
> ple estimate of the TWFE estimand. 
> However, weights can be negative. This is not relevant for the first regressi
> on (where Y is the outcome) as the ATT is the same for all states. But as we 
> get staggered adoption (Y2, Y3, Y4) negative weights matter, as this can lead
>  to wrong counterfactuals if the size or sign of the treatment changes over t
> ime. 
> In this simulation the cell (state = 2, year = 3) has the larger treatment ef
> fect as it both receives the baseline treatment and the positive shock: in th
> ese cases the ATT varies across units and time.
> The ATT for state 2, year 3 is way bigger but it is taken into account with a
>  negative weight: this leads the overall coefficient for D downward and even 
> makes it negative. As the size of the shock increases (progressively going fr
> om Y2 to Y4), the ATT for (state = 2, year = 3) increases, and the coefficien
> t for D gets more and more negative (the bias increases in absolute value). 
> Negative weights explain the biases in the last three regressions, and also w
> hy the bias increases as the shock increases. */
. 
. * POINT H
. 
. *reload data
. 
. use "${filepath}pset_2.dta", clear
(Divorce data from Wolfers (2006) AER)

. encode st, gen(st_id)

. xtset st_id year

Panel variable: st_id (strongly balanced)
 Time variable: year, 1956 to 1998
         Delta: 1 unit

. keep if inrange(year, 1956, 1988)
(510 observations deleted)

. gen IMP_UNILATERAL = 0 

. replace IMP_UNILATERAL = 1 if year >= lfdivlaw
(530 real changes made)

. 
. *create initial population variable
. sort st year

. by st: gen init_stpop = stpop[1]

. 
. *re-run regression using new weights
. reghdfe div_rate IMP_UNILATERAL [aw=init_stpop], absorb(st_id year) vce(clust
