. 
. *POINT K
. 
. *Wolfers (2006) produces a more exhaustive and dynamic analysis of data than 
> Friedberg (1998). His main contributions are on methdology and sample
. 
. * Methodology : Friedberg's state-specific trends are misidentified because s
> he uses a single Unilateral dummy to capture the full dynamic response, the s
> tate-specific trends end up absorbing part of the post-treatment adjustment (
> the return to baseline after the initial spike) rather than only pre-existing
>  differences. This leads her to overestimate the persistent effect of divorce
>  laws. Indeed "standard difference-in-difference approaches are led to confou
> nd pre-existing trends with the effects of the policy shock".
. * This is why in the exercise we add IMP_UNILATERAL, an absorbing variable.
. 
. * Sample : extending the pre-treatment period to 1956 allowing for a more cre
> dible identification of pre-existing state trends. He also investigates the p
> ost-treatment period to examine whether the treatment results in a shift of d
> ivorces timing rather than an increase in divorces. 
. 
. *Other contributions from his paper are: 
. *(i) adding some granularity on the analysis by constructing a measure for th
> e ever-divorced population and using the number of divorces per 1,000 married
>  persons as a metric
. * (ii) exploring implications for the stock of marriage through stock-flow dy
> namics. 
. *(iii) challenging Friedberg's conclusions on the long-term impact of divorce
>  laws on the divorce rate by showcasing the difficulty of such an analysis.
. * His conclusion is that the "results suggest only a minor role for changing 
> divorce laws", suggesting that spousal bargaing is "sufficiently close to eff
> icient"
. 
. * He explores two main assumptions:
. *(i) the Coasian assumption of efficient bargaining arguably provides a more 
> useful guide than the polar opposite assumption of no bargaining --> divorce 
> laws shouldn't affect divorce rate in the long-term.
. *(ii) each spouse has independent forecast errors.
. * His results are therefore consistent with a Coasian view of marital bargain
> ing. 
. 
. 
. * Point L
. 
. * Key requirements are satisfied, as we have some never-treated" or "last-tre
> ated" states to serve as the clean control.
. 
. * Graph 1 FE only
. 
. * Install these packages only if they are not already available.
. capture which avar

. if _rc ssc install avar

. capture which eventstudyinteract

. if _rc ssc install eventstudyinteract

. 
. * Step 1: generate control and treatment cohorts 
. gen never_treated = (lfdivlaw == 2000)

. gen cohort = lfdivlaw

. replace cohort = . if lfdivlaw == 2000
(660 real changes made, 660 to missing)

. 
. * Step 2: run Sun & Abraham estimator
. set matsize 11000
set matsize ignored.
    Matrix sizes are no longer limited by c(matsize) in modern Statas.
    Matrix sizes are now limited by edition of Stata.  See limits for more
    details.

